external:        Whether the issuer of the installed certificate is an approved certificate
                 authority - the product STIG verifies this by inspecting the certificate
                 with openssl on the host operating system; configuration records the trust
                 anchor's path, never its issuer
external:        Issuance of the server certificate and supply of the root CA public
                 certificate - the approved public key infrastructure
external:        Certificate revocation distribution and checking - the issuing PKI and its
                 CRL or OCSP service
external:        File system protection of the private key and certificate files -
                 operating system
external:        Certificate validation performed by a fronting reverse proxy or load
                 balancer that terminates TLS - that component's configuration

citation:        Product STIG SPLK-CL-000450 fix text: "Request a DOD-approved certificate
                 and a copy of the DOD root CA public certificate, and place the files in a
                 location for Splunk use. Configure the certificate files to the PEM
                 format"; its check text verifies the issuer by running openssl against the
                 certificate on the host operating system. server.conf.spec:714
                 ([sslConfig]) with :798-823 (sslVerifyServerCert = <boolean>, whether
                 splunkd as a client validates the certificate a server presents; default
                 false, and a false value means any valid certificate is acceptable),
                 :858-874 (requireClientCert, default false), :971-981 (serverCert, "The
                 Splunk daemon auto-generates certificates when you start Splunk Enterprise
                 the first time"; "replace the default certificate with a certificate that
                 you either create on your own or obtain from a third party"; default
                 $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/auth/server.pem), :996-1009 (sslRootCAPath, the
                 certificate authority or root certificate store, PEM format, no default);
                 outputs.conf.spec:700-708 ([tcpout] sslVerifyServerCert, default false);
                 web.conf.spec:82-91 (serverCert, default
                 $SPLUNK_HOME/etc/auth/splunkweb/cert.pem); inputs.conf.spec:876
                 ([splunktcp-ssl:<port>]) with :1015-1021 (requireClientCert). Excluded as
                 deprecated or out of stanza: server.conf.spec:1026-1032 (caCertFile,
                 "DEPRECATED. Use the 'sslRootCAPath' setting instead"),
                 inputs.conf.spec:1747-1748 (caCertFile, deprecated in favor of server.conf
                 sslRootCAPath), outputs.conf.spec:691-695 ([tcpout] sslRootCAPath,
                 deprecated in favor of server.conf sslRootCAPath), and
                 server.conf.spec:5392 (sslCRLPath, documented only under [kvstore] in this
                 release).
finding:         Certificate trust is not configured to the extent the product allows: the
                 root certificate authority store is unset, an auto-generated default
                 certificate is still in use on the splunkd or Splunk Web listener, server
                 certificate verification is disabled on connections the instance
                 establishes, or client certificates are not required on the receiving
                 port.
